// src/ast/ast_id.zen
// How a node is named.
//
// The four node families below are mutually recursive — an expression
// holds types, a type holds an expression, a pattern holds patterns —
// so a child is never held by value. It is held by an id into the
// matching arena in `Ast`, and `ast_arena.zen` is the only thing that
// turns one back into a node.
//
// Two reasons this is an id and not a `Ptr<Expr>`, and only the first
// is about layout:
//
//   1. `Node = { next: Node }` has no size, so recursion needs an
//      indirection whatever it is called.
//   2. "Sema is memoized queries, not monolithic passes. `type_of(node)`,
//      `defs_of(name)`" — DESIGN.md, decision 3. A memo table is a
//      `Map<K, V>` and `K` must be `Eq + Hash`, so a node reference has
//      to be a small value with an identity. A tree of values has no
//      identity at all and a raw pointer has no exported one, so an id
//      is the only shape in which that law can be obeyed.
//
// They are four types and not one `NodeId`, because passing a TypeId
// where an ExprId belongs is exactly the mistake four agents building
// against this in parallel are going to make, and it costs nothing to
// make it unrepresentable.
//
// An id is an INDEX, assigned in the order the parser creates nodes.
// That makes it a pure function of the source, which is what keeps
// gen_c deterministic: nothing here is an address.
//
// The index is a u32, not a usize, and that is forced rather than
// chosen. std.core.num carries only LOSSLESS conversions — a
// narrowing one can fail, so it belongs in a Res-returning function
// someone writes on purpose — and `u32` is the widest integer with
// both `to_usize` (to index the arena) and `to_u64` (to hash). A
// usize id could be neither indexed by a Map nor widened for a
// hasher. Four billion nodes is the cost, and it is not a cost.

Eq, Hash, Hasher = std.core.core

ExprId* = { index*: u32 }

TypeId* = { index*: u32 }

PatternId* = { index*: u32 }

BlockId* = { index*: u32 }

// Eq and Hash on all four, so a memoized query can key on a node. An
// impl lives with the type it is about, and these four types are
// declared here — std.core.eq could not have written them.
//
// The hash is the index itself: ids are dense and small, so mixing
// would be work spent hiding a distribution that is already perfect.
ExprId.impl(Eq, {
    eq ::= (self: @Self, other: @Self) bool { self.index == other.index }
})

ExprId.impl(Hash, {
    hash = (self: @Self, hasher :: Hasher) u64 { self.index.to_u64() }
})

TypeId.impl(Eq, {
    eq ::= (self: @Self, other: @Self) bool { self.index == other.index }
})

TypeId.impl(Hash, {
    hash = (self: @Self, hasher :: Hasher) u64 { self.index.to_u64() }
})

PatternId.impl(Eq, {
    eq ::= (self: @Self, other: @Self) bool { self.index == other.index }
})

PatternId.impl(Hash, {
    hash = (self: @Self, hasher :: Hasher) u64 { self.index.to_u64() }
})

BlockId.impl(Eq, {
    eq ::= (self: @Self, other: @Self) bool { self.index == other.index }
})

BlockId.impl(Hash, {
    hash = (self: @Self, hasher :: Hasher) u64 { self.index.to_u64() }
})
